.\" Generated from crates/writ-tool/src/specs.rs; do not edit.
.TH WRIT\-LEAF 1 2026-10-12 "writ 0.1.0" "writ manual"
.SH NAME
writ\-leaf \- make a short\-lived leaf key and grant tags to it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nh
.ad l
\fBwrit leaf\fR \fB\-i\fR\ \fIDIR\fR \fB\-\-out\fR\ \fIPATH\fR [\fB\-\-key\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]... [\fB\-\-grant\-out\fR\ \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-expires\fR\ \fIWHEN\fR] [\fB\-\-delegate\fR] [\fB\-\-note\fR\ \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] \fITAG\fR...
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Make a leaf key and grant each TAG to it. Leaf keys use ML\-DSA\-65 and Ed25519. They sign and verify quickly, and their signatures are smaller than an identity's. A leaf key lives at most 24 hours.
.PP
To keep using a leaf key, grant to its public key again (\fBwrit pubkey \-\-leaf KEY\fR prints it), or make a new one.
.PP
A leaf key can sign data. With \-\-delegate it can also issue grants, but those grants can't be delegated further.
.PP
A signature by a leaf key verifies only while the leaf key's grant is live, so use leaf keys for things that are signed again often, not for releases.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fITAG\fR
a list of words from general to specific, such as '(ssh nas)'; several TAGs make one grant that covers each of them
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-identity\fR \fIDIR\fR
the identity that signs the statement
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fIPATH\fR
secret key file (default: DIR/current.key); give one \-\-key per key when several keys must sign; '\-' means standard input
.TP
\fB\-\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
where to write the secret leaf key; '\-' means standard output
.TP
\fB\-\-grant\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
where to write the grant, a new file (default: standard output)
.TP
\fB\-\-expires\fR \fIWHEN\fR
how long the key lives, at most 24h (default: 24h)
.TP
\fB\-\-delegate\fR
let the leaf key grant TAG to others (who can't grant further)
.TP
\fB\-\-note\fR \fITEXT\fR
text to show with the statement; writ does not use it
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print only the grant
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print help; \fB\-h\fR is short, \fB\-\-help\fR is long
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
success; for questions, the answer is yes
.TP
1
the answer is no: the check, verification, proof or name lookup was denied
.TP
2
an error: bad usage, unreadable input, or a failed operation
.SH EXAMPLES
Make a key for a machine that signs a status file every hour:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ leaf \-i me '(status)' \-\-out ci.key >> bundle.writ
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwrit\fR(1), \fBwrit\-sign\fR(1), \fBwrit\-grant\fR(1), \fBwrit\-pubkey\fR(1)
